h one carries:"],[135,"Every generator value exposes `isValid()`. The remaining API matches the strategy. Time-ordered formats expose `timestamp()`. Formats with a canonical binary representation expose `toBytes()` and `fromBytes()`. CUID v2 and Nanoid expose a smaller API because they have no canonical byte representation. UUID v5 is the one generator that takes an input: it derives the same ID from the same name and namespace, and adds `namespace()` plus the RFC 9562 namespace constants."],[31,"Every generator-specific `--timestamp` option accepts a Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or `now`. ULID and TSID preserve millisecond precision. KSUID, ObjectID, and XID store whole seconds, so they discard any sub-second remainder from the millisecond input."],[230,"Every other generator creates a new value on each call. Sometimes you need the opposite: an ID that is a pure function of an identifier you already hold, such as a session ID issued by a third-party tool. The same input must give the same output in another process, on another machine, or after a reinstall, with no lookup table to keep."],[127,"Every strategy"],[251,"Every uniku generator beats its dedicated npm alternative in the current CI benchmark. See the measurements and bundle cost of each import."],[115,"Expected ID type (auto-detected if omitted)"],[476,"Export"],[131,"For a Deno project without a package import map, use Deno's npm specifier directly:"],[21,"For an exact pin, set `UNIKU_VERSION` to a version from the GitHub releases page, without the `uniku-cli-v` tag prefix."],[646,"For application names, pick your own namespace. Generate one UUID once, for example with `uuidv4()`, hard-code it, and never change it. A different namespace gives different IDs for every name."],[338,"From `@owpz/ksuid`"],[337,"From `@paralleldrive/cuid2`"],[339,"From `bson`"],[334,"From `nanoid`"],[340,"From `tsid-ts`"],[336,"From `typeid-js`"],[335,"From `ulid`"],[332,"From `uuid`"],[341,"From `xid-ts` or `nexid`"],[4,"Generate"],[72,"Generate CUIDs (v2)."],[79,"Generate KSUIDs."],[84,"Generate MongoDB ObjectIDs."],[50,"Generate monotonically increasing ULIDs"],[62,"Generate Nanoids."],[94,"Generate TSIDs: 64-bit Snowflake-style, time-sorted identifiers."],[55,"Generate TypeIDs: a UUID v7 with a type prefix."],[46,"Generate ULIDs."],[32,"Generate UUIDs (v4, v5, or v7)."],[89,"Generate XIDs compatible with rs/xid."],[2,"Generate, inspect, and validate IDs from the terminal."],[252,"Generation speed"],[255,"Generation speed is a primary design constraint in uniku. In the current CI snapshot, every strategy it measures beats the dedicated npm implementation it targets. The smallest measured lead is 1.2× for UUID v4 and ObjectID; ULID reaches 116×."],[259,"Generator"],[484,"Generator arguments are invalid: bad size, alphabet, length, timestamp, or version."],[470,"Generator list"],[441,"Generator references"],[123,"Getting started"],[217,"Go rs/xid compatibility"],[237,"Hono request IDs with Nanoid"],[241,"Hono's request ID middleware accepts a synchronous `generator`. When a request does not already carry a valid `X-Request-Id` header, this example generates a compact Nanoid, stores it in the request context, and returns it in the response header."],[440,"How modules differ"],[108,"ID type: `uuid`, `ulid`, `typeid`, `nanoid`, `cuid`, `ksuid`, `objectid`, `xid`, `tsid` (auto-detected if omitted)"],[133,"If you are used to class APIs, the attached helpers resemble \"static methods.\" The generator is still a function, so there is nothing to instantiate:"],[229,"If you create records in more than one service or need an ID before inserting a row, start with UUID v7. It is a UUID-native value, sort-friendly, and uses a familiar database column type."],[354,"Implied by which `isValid` matches: each accepts only its own version."],[285,"Import"],[447,"Import"],[492,"Import from `uniku/errors` when code handles failures from more than one generator. Individual generator entry points may also re-export the error classes they use."],[668,"Independent runtimes choose their identity randomly, so uniqueness across them remains probabilistic rather than host-anchored as in Go's `rs/xid` implementation."],[14,"Inspect"],[3,"Install"],[125,"Install"],[329,"Install @uniku/cli when you need an ID in a terminal, migration, or shell pipeline. It shares the library's formats but has its own release lifecycle."],[124,"Install eleven type-safe ID strategies, then import only the generator your application needs."],[17,"Installs the current standalone binary without requiring Node.js:"],[235,"Integrations"],[331,"Keep familiar formats while moving to direct, portable imports."],[10,"ksuid"],[170,"KSUID"],[273,"KSUID"],[602,"KSUID"],[604,"KSUID combines a timestamp with a large random payload. Choose it for high-volume distributed systems that need chronological order and a 27-character URL-safe string."],[68,"Length of the ID, 1-256 (default: `21`)"],[78,"Length of the ID, 2-32 (default: `24`)"],[216,"Matches MongoDB's 12-byte ObjectID format."],[219,"Matches rs/xid's compact 12-byte text and binary format."],[498,"Meaning"],[330,"Migrate from other libraries"],[371,"Migrate to uniku v1"],[342,"Migration is usually an import change first. Keep the stored identifier format stable, then move generation behind the new direct entry point. Every example below shows the value returned by the uniku generator."],[286,"Minified + gzipped"],[214,"MongoDB `_id` compatibility"],[610,"MongoDB-compatible 12-byte identifiers."],[643,"Most callers use one namespace for many names. `uuidv5.namespace()` validates and parses the namespace once and returns a function that takes only the name:"],[373,"Move CUID v2 to the versioned entry point"],[506,"Mutually exclusive compatibility and canonical options were supplied together."],[40,"Name to derive a UUID v5 from, hashed as UTF-8 (requires `-v 5`)"],[333,"Name-based UUID v5"],[631,"Name-based UUIDs derived from a namespace and a name, with no state to keep."],[8,"nanoid"],[166,"Nanoid"],[209,"Nanoid"],[271,"Nanoid"],[605,"Nanoid"],[277,"Nanoid (10 chars)"],[607,"Nanoid is the compact choice for short URLs, invite codes, and session tokens. Its default alphabet is URL-safe; pass a length or options only when the boundary needs a different shape."],[220,"Native `BIGINT` storage"],[119,"No output, exit code only (`0` valid, `2` invalid)"],[637,"No timestamp"],[100,"Node ID (`0` to `2^node-bits - 1`)"],[213,"Non-sequential, secure identifier."],[436,"Non-sequential, string-native identifiers that resist enumeration."],[28,"Number of IDs to generate (default: `1`)"],[11,"objectid"],[174,"ObjectID"],[215,"ObjectID"],[281,"ObjectID"],[609,"ObjectID"],[611,"ObjectID matches MongoDB's native 12-byte representation. Use it when your application stores `_id` values alongside MongoDB clients or needs to preserve that wire format."],[23,"Option"],[33,"Option"],[47,"Option"],[56,"Option"],[63,"Option"],[73,"Option"],[80,"Option"],[85,"Option"],[90,"Option"],[95,"Option"],[105,"Option"],[112,"Option"],[30,"Output as a JSON array instead of one ID per line"],[110,"Output as JSON"],[121,"Output as JSON"],[44,"Output in lowercase"],[54,"Output in lowercase"],[250,"Performance and bundle size"],[382,"Pre-v1 code(s)"],[379,"Pre-v1 error codes included the generator name. V1 uses strategy-agnostic codes and reports the generator separately through `error.strategy`:"],[372,"Prepare for the v1 entry-point contract without changing identifier data."],[141,"Primary value"],[205,"Public API resources"],[191,"Public inputs are validated. Explicit random bytes and buffer offsets make tests and boundary integrations deterministic without changing the generator's default monotonic state."],[448,"Purpose"],[627,"Random, standards-compatible UUIDs with 122 bits of entropy."],[117,"Read IDs from stdin (one per line)"],[453,"Read the reference for the module you use. Each Public API section is generated from the TypeScript signatures and JSDoc in `packages/uniku/src`, so it stays aligned with the library source."],[434,"Read the stability contract for the full release and runtime policy."],[254,"Reproduce these numbers"],[245,"Run it with `pnpm --filter @uniku/examples example:drizzle`."],[248,"Run it with `pnpm --filter @uniku/examples example:effect`."],[242,"Run it with `pnpm --filter @uniku/examples example:hono`."],[316,"Run the benchmark and bundle-size tooling from the repository root:"],[236,"Runnable starting points for Hono, Drizzle ORM v1, and Effect v4."],[186,"See Choosing an ID for the trade-offs, or open a generator reference when you already know which format you need."],[432,"Several old codes intentionally converge on one v1 meaning. This only changes error handling; generated and persisted identifier data is unaffected."],[651,"SHA-1 is fine for identity. It is not a security boundary: someone who controls the names can craft two that collide. uniku has no SHA-256 or UUID v8 variant. If names come from an untrusted party and a collision would matter, do not rely on the ID alone."],[208,"Short URLs and invite codes"],[199,"Situation"],[624,"Sortable, URL-safe identifiers using Crockford Base32."],[318,"Start here"],[497,"Strategies"],[138,"Strategy"],[189,"String-native formats do not pretend otherwise. Nanoid and CUID v2 have no canonical byte encoding, so they expose a smaller API."],[635,"Strings are hashed as UTF-8, without normalization"],[656,"Such a TypeID has no timestamp: `typeid.timestamp()` throws an `InvalidInputError` with code `UUID_NOT_V7`."],[243,"The `binaryUlid` custom type exposes a string to application code while declaring a PostgreSQL `bytea` column. Its Drizzle codec converts ULID strings to `Uint8Array` parameters and selected bytes back to canonical ULID strings."],[642,"The arguments are `(name, namespace)`, in the same order as the `uuid` package. Both accept a string or a `Uint8Array`. Like every uniku generator, `uuidv5()` is synchronous: the SHA-1 implementation is small and inlined, so the entry point has no runtime dependency and imports no other generator."],[644,"The bound function holds the namespace, not past results. It copies namespace bytes, so changing your array afterwards does not change the IDs it derives."],[452,"The canonical ordered list of supported generator kinds."],[256,"The claim is deliberately scoped to generation speed against the established package for each format. Application performance still depends on where and how IDs are created."],[244,"The column's `$defaultFn` generates a ULID when an insert omits `id`. Both sides of the foreign key remain 16-byte values in PostgreSQL, so the join compares their binary representation while the returned `eventId` is already a string. The example uses an in-memory PGlite database, so it needs no external PostgreSQL server."],[283,"The compatibility benchmark runs each implementation in a fresh process. CI balances both launch orders across four repetitions and reports the per-row median, so warmed runtime state and one noisy run do not decide the comparison."],[225,"The conventional random UUID format."],[315,"The CUID v2 entry point imports SHA3-512 from `@noble/hashes`, uniku's one runtime dependency; the size above excludes that external dependency's own weight. The UUID v5 entry point inlines its SHA-1, so its size includes the whole hash and it has no runtime dependency."],[667,"The default identity is random and cached for this runtime. Supplying `machineId` or `processId` changes only that field; calls still use the shared counter unless you also supply `counter`. Supply all four fields for deterministic output."],[246,"The example pins Drizzle ORM `1.0.0-rc.4`. Codecs are configured per PostgreSQL type, so this client treats every `bytea` column as a binary ULID. The v1 line is still a release candidate, so check its release notes before changing that version."],[240,"The examples below add Nanoid request IDs to Hono, binary ULIDs to a Drizzle schema, and prefixed TypeIDs to an Effect service. Each is self-contained and runs with the command below it."],[494,"The following codes form the v1 public contract:"],[378,"The generator behavior is the same. This is an import migration, not a data migration: existing CUID v2 values remain valid."],[20,"The installer resolves the latest CLI release automatically. Set `UNIKU_INSTALL_DIR` to choose a destination:"],[320,"The library contract"],[132,"The named generator import has two roles. Call it to generate an ID. Use the same value as a dictionary of methods and constants for that strategy."],[446,"The package root is intentionally not exported, so import a generator or metadata module directly. Two metadata entry points round out the library:"],[344,"The rest of `uuid`'s API maps onto uniku's per-generator methods, scoped to the version you imported:"],[599,"The same code can be used by more than one error class when the failure meaning is identical. For example, `TIMESTAMP_OUT_OF_RANGE` can describe an invalid generation option or an overflow discovered while parsing."],[228,"The same name and namespace always give the same UUID, with no state to keep."],[104,"The type is auto-detected unless `--type` is given. For time-ordered IDs (UUID v7, ULID, TypeID, KSUID, ObjectID, XID, TSID), inspect extracts the embedded timestamp. A TypeID that wraps another UUID version, such as one built with `typeid.fromUuid()` from a UUID v5, reports its version and no timestamp. Auto-detection treats a prefixless TypeID as a ULID unless it wraps a UUID v7. Random-only IDs (UUID v4, CUID v2, Nanoid) and name-based UUID v5 IDs report that no decodable metadata is available. Timestamp precision matches each generator: UUID v7, ULID, and TSID are millisecond-precision; KSUID, ObjectID, and XID are second-precision."],[126,"The usual shape"],[249,"This example pins Effect `4.1.3`. Check the Effect release notes before changing that version."],[188,"This matters when an ID lives in a binary database column, a compact protocol payload, or a cache key. A UUID, for instance, is 16 bytes instead of its 36-character string form. The helpers also make the string boundary explicit: convert when data leaves your application, preserve bytes where the system already handles bytes."],[258,"This snapshot comes from a four-repetition CI run on July 14, 2026. UUID v5 was added after this snapshot, so it has no row yet. Treat the ratios as directional because shared-runner noise and dependency updates can move them. The rolling baseline tracks results across CI runs for regression detection."],[444,"This table is derived from the generated TypeScript API reference. Open a module's page for complete signatures, options, constants, and examples."],[381,"This table maps every machine-readable code emitted by `uniku@0.4.3` to its v1 replacement:"],[478,"Thrown when"],[197,"Time order leaks creation time"],[196,"Time ordering is local"],[603,"Time-ordered identifiers with a 128-bit random payload."],[662,"Time-ordered UUIDs for primary keys, APIs, and event streams."],[204,"Time-ordered values improve index locality."],[613,"Time-sorted 64-bit identifiers for native bigint storage."],[618,"Timestamps"],[13,"tsid"],[182,"TSID"],[221,"TSID"],[269,"TSID"],[612,"TSID"],[614,"TSID returns a `bigint`, not a string. Use it when a database `BIGINT` primary key is the natural representation and you still need time ordering without a central allocator."],[61,"Type prefix, e.g. `user` for `user_...` (empty by default)"],[467,"Typed, machine-readable errors for invalid input, malformed strings, and buffer bounds."],[450,"Typed, machine-readable input, parse, and buffer errors."],[7,"typeid"],[158,"TypeID"],[206,"TypeID"],[267,"TypeID"],[615,"TypeID"],[619,"TypeID puts a lowercase prefix such as `user_` or `order_` in front of a base32-encoded UUID. It is useful when a public identifier should reveal the kind of resource it identifies. `typeid()` always generates a UUID v7, so new TypeIDs sort by creation time."],[6,"ulid"],[154,"ULID"],[261,"ULID"],[623,"ULID"],[625,"ULIDs sort by creation time and stay shorter than a hyphenated UUID. Use them when the value will often appear in URLs, logs, or APIs."],[346,"uniku"],[357,"uniku"],[364,"uniku also exposes `uuidv5.OID` and `uuidv5.X500`, and `uuidv5.namespace(ns)` to parse a namespace once for many names. See UUID v5."],[321,"uniku collects eleven ID generation strategies behind one consistent, type-safe API: UUID v4/v5/v7, ULID, TypeID, Nanoid, CUID v2, KSUID, ObjectID, XID, and TSID. In the current CI benchmark, every benchmarked generator beats its dedicated npm alternative. Each strategy still has its own entry point, so your application ships only the code it imports."],[343,"uniku generates UUID v4, v5, and v7. There is no uniku equivalent for v1, v3, or v6; keep the `uuid` package for those, or generate them elsewhere and only use uniku for v4, v5, and v7 values."],[130,"uniku is ESM-only. Import the exact generator you want rather than a package-root barrel:"],[654,"uniku keeps no cache. If a hot loop derives the same names repeatedly, cache the results in your own code, with a size bound you choose."],[376,"uniku v1 keeps its canonical identifier formats stable while finalizing its pre-v1 entry points and machine-readable error contract."],[260,"uniku vs npm"],[52,"Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or `now`"],[83,"Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or `now`"],[88,"Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or `now`"],[93,"Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or `now`"],[98,"Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or `now`"],[374,"Update error-code matches"],[122,"Use `--json` whenever the output feeds another program. The CLI and library publish independently: a library `1.0.0` release does not imply a CLI `1.0.0` release at the same time."],[629,"Use `toBytes()` when the destination stores UUIDs as 16-byte binary values. Use `isValid()` before accepting an unknown UUID v4 string at an application boundary."],[369,"Use `xid.toBytes(id)` and `xid.fromBytes(bytes)` when you store the 12-byte value."],[140,"Usual function call"],[5,"uuid"],[616,"UUID identifiers with a readable, domain-specific prefix, generated as UUID v7."],[142,"UUID v4"],[224,"UUID v4"],[279,"UUID v4"],[626,"UUID v4"],[628,"UUID v4 is the default when compatibility matters more than sort order. It fits databases and APIs that already understand UUID strings."],[146,"UUID v5"],[227,"UUID v5"],[630,"UUID v5"],[641,"UUID v5 derives an ID from something you already have. It hashes a namespace and a name with SHA-1 (RFC 9562, section 5.5), so the same inputs give the same ID in every process, on every runtime, a year later. Use it when you receive an identifier from a system you do not control and want your own stable ID for it."],[231,"UUID v5 does this. It hashes a namespace and a name, so the function is the table:"],[45,"UUID v5 is name-based: the same `--name` and `--namespace` always produce the same ID. With `-v 5`, both `--name` and `--namespace` are required and `--count` cannot exceed `1`. Passing `--name` or `--namespace` with `-v 4` or `-v 7` is an error, as is a `--namespace` that is neither an alias nor a well-formed UUID."],[42,"UUID v5 namespace: `dns`, `url`, `oid`, `x500` (case-insensitive), or any UUID string (requires `-v 5`)"],[150,"UUID v7"],[263,"UUID v7"],[661,"UUID v7"],[663,"UUID v7 keeps the UUID shape while embedding a millisecond timestamp. It is a strong default for database primary keys when you want chronological locality without creating a custom ID column."],[203,"UUID v7 or ULID"],[207,"UUID v7 with a readable, domain-specific prefix."],[234,"UUID v7, ULID, KSUID, ObjectID, XID, and TSID embed a creation timestamp in the identifier itself. Anyone who can read the ID can decode when the record was created, not just its order relative to other records. That is a feature for index locality and a leak if the ID is public and the creation time is sensitive, for example a document, invoice, or account that should not reveal how old it is. If the identifier is exposed outside your system and creation time must stay private, use UUID v4, CUID v2, or Nanoid instead: none of them encode a timestamp."],[233,"UUID v7, ULID, ObjectID, XID, and TSID carry time information. uniku keeps the state required for monotonic sequencing inside one JavaScript isolate; it does not coordinate clocks or sequences between processes or machines."],[38,"UUID version: `4`, `5`, or `7` (default: `4`)"],[187,"UUIDs, ULIDs, TypeIDs, KSUIDs, ObjectIDs, XIDs, and TSIDs have a canonical binary representation. Their `toBytes()` and `fromBytes()` helpers let you store and transport that representation without inventing a second codec."],[383,"V1 code"],[15,"Validate"],[211,"Values that should resist enumeration"],[375,"What remains stable"],[201,"Why"],[128,"Why byte helpers exist"],[638,"Why not memoize a random ID"],[433,"Within `1.x`, uniku will not remove or rename documented entry points, exports, options, methods, or constants. Canonical string formats, byte order, timestamp units, and documented error codes are part of the contract."],[617,"Wrapping an existing UUID"],[129,"Write into a caller-owned buffer"],[12,"xid"],[178,"XID"],[218,"XID"],[275,"XID"],[664,"XID"],[666,"XID is a 12-byte, lowercase base32hex identifier: a seconds-precision timestamp, a 40-bit per-runtime identity, and a shared 24-bit counter. Use it when you need Go's rs/xid wire format in a synchronous JavaScript runtime."],[134,"You do not import `toBytes()` or `timestamp()` separately. They stay attached to `uuidv7` and grouped with that strategy."]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":12,"2":13,"3":14,"4":15,"5":16,"6":17,"7":18,"8":19,"9":20,"10":21,"11":22,"12":23,"13":24,"14":25,"15":26,"16":27,"17":28,"18":29,"19":30,"20":31,"21":32,"22":33,"23":34,"24":35,"25":36,"26":37,"27":38,"28":39,"29":40,"30":41,"31":42,"32":43,"33":44,"34":45,"35":46,"36":47,"37":48,"38":49,"39":50,"4